thank you dr narayan for your  kind introduction today's topic as  mentioned would be  molecular testing for the detection and  strain identification of science kov2  i thought i'd put a small introduction  in here about coronaviruses which i  think most of you know what it's about  there are several coronaviruses that  have been around and discovered in the  last 200 years  many of them in domestic animals there  are about  seven of them in humans four are pretty  benign  and have been around causing mild colds  we saw the first  pathogenic more pathogenic strain come  out in 2003 or so with the sarcoid  and then we had the most coronaviruses  and more recently as you all know the  major pandemic with the  the sars kobe 2. so the life cycle for  these viruses is listed in here and i  don't intend to go into details just to  say that they are  single-stranded rna viruses positive  strain  they enter the cell using the ace  receptor and  a protein called s-protein that's on the  surface of  the virus and we'll talk about some of  the mutations in  the s gene and in fact nonviolent  pathogenesis and revolution  uh to combat this thermo fisher  is a worldwide organization and we  have put in a lot of tools and  surveillance techniques to aid  laboratories worldwide we have many  innovative offerings in the kobe testing  space  we also have methods to do reflex  testing for identification of variants  by  sanger sequencing ngs and genotyping  assets as you see there is a diagnostic  component here and then if there are  variants that need to be detected you  can go the  rtpcr route or use anger or ngs  i'm only going to talk about the  detection format and the qpcr  genotyping format today so when  we started off with the pandemic early  in march  there was not much sequence data  available  we therefore decided that to be very  safe we would target three regions of  the virus  a current diagnostic kid i say targets  the orf1 the sg  and the engine so that gives us a lot of  redundancy  in terms of being i would call it  mutation proofing the essay  so variations in one region will still  not  reduce the effectiveness of this essay  so  multiple targeted regions we have a lot  of bioinformatics tools and algorithms  to make sure that  we have extensive strain coverage near  100  we have done a lot of testing in ours to  ensure that the multiplexing is done  right with  design of experiments and other tools  that  are important for ensuring essay  robustness  we also have tested their say for  robustness stability and failure modes  and then put it through an eua  application with  fda and it's been approved and out there  for quite some time  our so we have a large number of systems  out there  and that's one of our strengths in the  sense that there's about  six different instrument formats out  there you're seeing three of them in  here  additionally we have a huge  manufacturing capability  so all of which has been tremendously  useful in making sure that  our essays are distributed worldwide and  not only across the u.s but in many  countries almost all countries in the  world  the workflow for the detection essay is  fairly straightforward we have a  kingfisher  instrument that can be used to extract  the rna from the sample  the eluted material is then set up in an  rt pcr reaction  we have software to analyze the  data that comes out of the instrument in  terms of fluorescence signals  that leads into the code interpretive  software and you get a defined  interpretation  of positive negative and in some cases  some inconclusives  there are controls in there to monitor  process controls to monitor the entire  workflow  and we provide a clear interpretation to  the customers  based on positive calls for at least two  out of the three genes in here  if you get a single gene positive it's  called an inconclusive and you're asked  to  repeat the test we do provide ct values  in here  that are not only a measure of positive  detection but  also can be used to estimate  in a rough manner the amount of virus  sits around  a couple of things we did we have done  several studies comparing our essay with  other essays out there in the market  this is one such study  where we compared it to the the  logic panther fusion and the kobas  essays  as you can see the positive agreement is  around 100  to the negative percent agreement is on  97.5 percent is pretty good concordance  for an essay like this  we did uh test retest some of the  discarded samples  which come up in this study using sanger  sequencing and  as you can see five out of six were  concordant with  what we had and one out of six was  concorded with the rosh cobas essay  and most of these discarded samples  actually come up  in the city range near lod around 33 34  and you know as you know they can work  differently on different days depending  on how much material gets into your  assay so that's  pretty good in terms of performance we  have also been tracking  emerging variants one of the essays in  our  triplex is an sg sa and this  had a deletion in the s gene did impact  this essay  it turned out that this deletion was a  biomarker  in the uk the southern region uk for  reflex testing  the deletion is a marker for the b117  not all deletions are b117 but a large  percentage of them in the southern  region of kent  or in the uk turned up to correlate with  the deletion  so this was useful for a large number of  labs to track this  there are other key variants out there  now the south african variant the  brazilian variant and a few others as  you'll see in the next slide  this is what i took out of a  presentation at the who  it clearly shows that there are  mutations  associated with particular variants from  the uk  south africa and the brazilian variants  one of the things i'd like to point out  is one of these mutations e484k  was not initially found in the variants  that you see in the uk but it's now  there  so these mutations do move around  depending on  you know viral recombinations or other  events or infections  so you're starting to see some of the  mutations in the african strain show up  in  the uk in the b117 background and i  think this needs a lot more study as it  move  moves ahead there's a recent paper from  julio de oliveira's lab in south africa  quoting six other variants that are in  in play so potential implications for  the sarsko to violence is that some of  them  have increased human human transmission  as documented with the 117  they could potentially have different  disease severity  the susceptibility to therapeutic agents  may vary especially monoclonal  antibiotics and  vaccination protocols and they can evade  vaccine immunity so all of this has to  be  taken into account and from that point  of view tracking these variants is going  to be useful  as you can see the data here on this  slide there's some data coming out of  functional studies with human serum  indicating  that the b117 variant  is neutralized by antibodies generated  by  this is a study with the moderna vaccine  whereas there is some  level of decrease in utilization  capability when it comes to  the african variant it's about a six  volt decrease  the authors conclude that the vaccine  will still work well enough to  neutralize  the south african strain as well but we  have to await  other studies that will come up with  other vaccines and some of it is out  there in the  in the public as well  in terms of mutation detection we came  up with the genotyping format  where we have essays that contain two  probes one  is a probe in the fam channel that picks  up a variant and the other one is  a probe in the wick channel that picks  up the wild type and as you can see here  in this this particular plot the wild  type sequences come up for  for a particular variant and i mean the  mutant sequences come up for a  particular variant and the wild type  detection is in below detection limits  we can also use these cluster plots  to define the variant coming up here  from b117 the n501y and b117  and the wild type sequence is coming in  here now this data came to us from  unfortunately that got deleted out came  to us from mark adams at  the jackson laboratory institute so we'd  like to give him credit for that work  we also have data coming to us from  laboratories in the uk for different  mutations as you can see here the same  n501 variant so we pick up the variant  in one channel and we pick up the wild  type in the other channel  so these can be done by real-time plots  or preferably  using these allelic discrimination plots  on two axis  one for one channel one for the other  as you can see here this is another  mutation that's been coming up with the  literature  called n439k this mutation was  flagged in recent cell paper as being  something  that is required for the s protein to  take on  additional variations it's in a large  number of variants  predates the appearance of some of the  mutations like the n501  the e484k that we're all more familiar  with  so again this data is also very early  and a lot of this  is going to come up in more detail down  the road  two other examples here so this is a  deletion e484k  and this one is the figure for the  deletion 6970 that we see in the b117s  the 484k the 510y and the 417  the k417 and are considered to be  critical variants in close to the  receptor binding domain in the s protein  that could be critical  in terms of evading vaccine response the  three of them together  in the south african strain is therefore  much more of a concern  than right now in terms of what's in the  b117  but these variants i believe will evolve  in the future and we may have  you know many more mutations that come  up in these different strains  but critically this is data from our  internal studies where we use synthetic  templates for the wild type and the  variant  and this is studies from the lab in  europe that actually use variants to  separate out the variant call from the  wi-fi call  so we're trying to collect as much data  as we can so we know that these essays  out in the market are robust  from an uh applied by assistant  thermofisher  point of view we have multiple solutions  again as i mentioned  we have the rtpcr qpcr essays that  i talked about for detection and  diagnostics  we have mutation verification essays on  the qpcr formats  which i just talked about showed your  data the genotyping format we can also  do this  by single sequencing capillary  electrophoresis  and then in addition to that if you need  whole genome sequences  you could do it with the saga formats or  you could do it without ion tolerant  formats for  strain surveillance and there's a lot of  emphasis  on that especially in the uk they're  sequencing about five percent of the  variance  just to get a good feel for what the  distribution is  the us is behind on this right now but  i'm sure we will catch up  in the next few months so in summary  the global pandemic due to stars  has infected millions of people as you  know around the world resulted in many  deaths  an additional concern has been the  emergence of variants across the globe  that some of them have increased trans  transmissibility  some may potentially cause higher  disease severity and  obviously you have the possibility that  they escape neutralization by  antibodies so garments pharmaceutical  companies diagnostic companies  have all risen to the challenge to  contain the pandemic by introducing  large-scale testing  test and trade trace plans and  surveillance of genetic  variants and thermo fisher has been  particularly a big part of it  specifically enable the use of  different platforms that we have for  both the detection and genotyping of the  virus  we have also introduced some antibody  based testing solutions and work to  enable  sample collection and vaccine production  effects which i didn't talk about  in this particular presentation here so  with  that i'll stop

